## Proof

**Setup and strategy.** Model Martin Luther Street as a line with the mathematicians' starting corner (Barbarossa) at the origin $0$, and Meininger Street at an unknown signed integer position $x$ with $|x|=N$ (they don't know the sign or the magnitude). A "strategy" is a rule telling them, whenever they are at the origin, how far to walk in each direction before turning back. We may assume WLOG that an optimal strategy has the following *excursion form*: a sequence of trips $a_1,a_2,a_3,\dots$ where trip $i$ goes distance $a_i$ in direction $(-1)^{i+1}$ (alternating right/left) and then returns to the origin (except possibly the last trip, which stops when the destination is reached). Any strategy can be replaced by one of this form without increasing the worst-case cost, since extra wandering between excursions only adds distance.

We prove the optimal competitive ratio is exactly $9$, so the smallest integer $k$ with the required guarantee is $\boxed{9}$.

---

### Upper bound: $k=9$ suffices (doubling strategy)

Use the doubling strategy $a_i = 2^{i-1}$: go $1$ right, return; $2$ left, return; $4$ right, return; $8$ left, return; …

Suppose the destination lies to the **right** at distance $N>0$ (the left case is symmetric). It is discovered during the first rightward excursion reaching it. Rightward excursions are trips $1,3,5,\dots$ with lengths $1,4,16,\dots,4^{j},\dots$. So the destination is found in trip $2j+1$ where $j$ is the smallest index with $4^{j}\ge N$, i.e. $4^{j-1}<N\le 4^{j}$.

Total distance walked before trip $2j+1$ begins equals
$$
2\sum_{i=1}^{2j} a_i \;=\; 2(1+2+4+\cdots+2^{2j-1}) \;=\; 2(2^{2j}-1)=2(4^{j}-1).
$$
During trip $2j+1$ they walk exactly $N$ (stopping at the destination). Hence the total cost is
$$
C(N)=2(4^{j}-1)+N.
$$
Using $4^{j}<4N$ (since $4^{j-1}<N$),
$$
C(N)=2\cdot 4^{j}-2+N < 2\cdot 4N -2 + N = 9N-2 < 9N.
$$
Thus $C(N)<9N$ for every $N$, so $k=9$ is achievable.

---

### Lower bound: $k<9$ is impossible

Consider any excursion-form strategy with (nondecreasing, unbounded) lengths $a_1,a_2,\dots$ alternating directions. For the search to terminate for every target, we need $a_i\to\infty$ and the excursions to eventually cover every distance on both sides.

Place a target on the side of trip $i$ at distance $a_i+\varepsilon$ (just beyond the reach of trip $i$). This target is **not** found by trip $i$; it is found by the next trip on the same side, namely trip $i+2$, after trips $i$ and $i+1$ have been completed and the walker has returned to the origin. The cost is
$$
C_i \;=\; 2\sum_{m=1}^{i+1} a_m \;+\; (a_i+\varepsilon).
$$
For a competitive ratio $\le c$ we must have, for every $i$ and arbitrarily small $\varepsilon>0$,
$$
2\sum_{m=1}^{i+1} a_m + a_i \;\le\; c\,a_i,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
2\sum_{m=1}^{i+1} a_m \;\le\; (c-1)\,a_i. \tag{$*$}
$$

Since $\sum_{m=1}^{i+1} a_m \ge a_{i+1}$, ($*$) gives $2a_{i+1}\le (c-1)a_i$, so the growth ratio $r_i:=a_{i+1}/a_i$ satisfies $r_i\le (c-1)/2$. For the search to reach arbitrarily far we need $r_i>1$ eventually, so $(c-1)/2>1$, i.e. $c>3$ — a first weak bound.

To get the sharp bound, sum the geometric tail. From ($*$),
$$
2\Bigl(a_{i+1}+\sum_{m=1}^{i}a_m\Bigr)\le (c-1)a_i.
$$
Applying ($*$) at index $i-1$ gives $2\sum_{m=1}^{i}a_m\le (c-1)a_{i-1}$, so $\sum_{m=1}^{i}a_m\le \tfrac{c-1}{2}a_{i-1}$. Substituting,
$$
2a_{i+1} + (c-1)a_{i-1} \;\le\; (c-1)a_i,
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad
2a_{i+1}\le (c-1)(a_i-a_{i-1}). \tag{$**$}
$$
Set $r:=\limsup a_{i+1}/a_i$ (the asymptotic growth rate; it exists along a subsequence attaining the worst case). Dividing ($**$) by $a_i$ and using $a_{i-1}/a_i\to 1/r$,
$$
2r \;\le\; (c-1)\Bigl(1-\tfrac{1}{r}\Bigr)=\frac{(c-1)(r-1)}{r},
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
2r^{2}\le (c-1)(r-1). \tag{$***$}
$$
Inequality ($***$) requires the quadratic $2r^{2}-(c-1)r+(c-1)\le 0$ to have a real root with $r>1$. Its discriminant is
$$
\Delta=(c-1)^{2}-8(c-1)=(c-1)(c-9).
$$
For a real root we need $\Delta\ge 0$, i.e. $(c-1)(c-9)\ge 0$. Since $c>3>1$, this forces
$$
c\ge 9.
$$
Therefore **no** strategy can achieve competitive ratio $c<9$. In particular, for any strategy there exists some $N$ with $C(N)\ge 9N>8N$, so $k=8$ (and every smaller integer) is impossible.

---

### Conclusion

The doubling strategy guarantees strictly fewer than $9N$ blocks for every $N$, so $k=9$ works. The lower bound shows every strategy has competitive ratio at least $9$, so no $k<9$ can work. Hence the smallest positive integer with the required guarantee is

$$
\boxed{k=9}.
$$

